2 layer prints the ornamental initial as `l^c-ce` and the syllabified chant as `li - gnum Cru - cis`. Corroboration: none attempted.","relationship":"editorial-projection-exact-to-target","source_date":"2026-09-03 project editorial projection; antecedent Pustet Ratisbon 1862","source_id":"passage.triptych.roman-1962-latin-proper-editorial-projection.editorial-projection-2026-09-03.good-friday-showing-of-the-cross","status":"collated","transformations":["join printed line wraps","remove stress accents","expand printed ae/oe ligatures to ae/oe","normalise consonantal i to the calendar's j orthography","apply the target printing's punctuation","carry the target's conclusion at its printed length"],"verification_locator":"printed p. 176, no. 951, under the heading ANTIPHONA AD DETEGENDAM S. CRUCEM; artifact PDF p. 257","verification_source_id":"passage.catholic-church.missale-romanum.vatican-typica-1962.temporal-good-friday-orations"},"publication":{"basis":"public-domain","evidence":"The wording is preexisting material of the 1962 typical edition under 17 U.S.C. 103(b), which gives a revised edition no exclusive right in material it did not contribute. The independent registered public-domain witness is the tracked 1862 Pustet text artifact. The position is settled for the repository at src/sources/inventories/missale-romanum-1962-facsimile-rights-v1.toml, which names the seasonal orations as preexisting material and requires exactly this pairing of 103(b) with a public-domain witness.","locator":"Pustet Ratisbon 1862, printed p. 173 (Feria VI. in Parasceve, at the unveiling of the Cross); text-layer lines 32857-32872, with the governing rubric at 32830-32854; artifact rights_status public-domain on a book first published in 1862","source_ids":["artifact.triptych.roman-1962-latin-proper-editorial-projection.editorial-projection-2026-09-03.triduum-orations-21aa7a98","artifact.catholic-church.missale-romanum.pustet-ratisbon-1862.missale-romanum-1862-text-f34bc7cf"],"status":"permitted","surfaces":["cli","corpus-data","download","print","public-git","web"]},"reason":null,"text_sha256":"3b6541edfc86861ae0dba7a0b61e5454cac6d46b2874448e097c16983a90a5d8","withheld":false},"name":"Showing of the Cross","source":"composed","taken_from":null,"text":"Ecce lignum Crucis, in quo salus mundi pependit. Omnes R. Venite, adoremus.\n","text_status":null,"translations":[{"caution":"A pre-1955 lay hand missal, not an approved liturgical translation and never an altar book. Its rubrics, its seasonal second and third orations, its Holy Week and its sanctoral are not those of 1962. It is carried only where a 1962 formulary is the same prayer, and nothing here may be used for recitation.\n\nThe 1862 prints `Ecce lignum Crucis, in quo salus mundi pependit. Chorus R. Venite adoremus.` with the same threefold repetition the 1962 keeps; the 1861 prints the Latin response `Venite adoremus` beside its own English, so the identity is visible inside the witness as well. The 1955 order moved the unveiling to three stations, which changes the ceremony and not these words. Page-image collation confirms that this historical English body answers the complete target Latin identity; publication is limited to the 1861 wording and does not claim that Cummiskey appoints the 1962 celebration.","lang":"en","rights":"public-domain","source_id":"edition.eugene-cummiskey.roman-missal-english-laity.philadelphia-1861","source_label":"The Roman Missal translated into the English language for the use of the laity, first revised edition (Philadelphia: Eugene Cummiskey, 1861)","text":"Behold the wood of the cross, on which hung the Salvation of the World. R. Come let us adore."}],"unavailable_translations":null,"untranslated":null},{"citations":[{"book":"Micah","loci":{"hebrew":[{"chapter":6,"first":3,"last":4}],"hebrew-unnumbered-titles":[{"chapter":6,"first":3,"last":4}],"vulgate":[{"chapter":6,"first":3,"last":4}]},"ref":"Micah 6:3-4","token":"Mich","unresolved":null},{"book":"I